  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nUnder the influence of a blocking high, Central Illinois will \nsee stable conditions persist through midday Tuesday while a \nNor'easter slowly moves along the Mid-Atlantic and New England \ncoasts. High temperatures are expected to stay in the upper 60s \nto mid-70s, accompanied by occasional low clouds and a slight \npossibility of sprinkles.\n\nBy Tuesday evening, global deterministic models indicate the \nridge will begin breaking down as a shortwave trough advances \ninto the Four Corners region. Guidance predominantly suggests \nthis feature will merge with the tropical remnants of Polo. If \nthis scenario holds, deep southwesterly flow ahead of the system\nwill spread across the Southern Plains and Mid-Mississippi \nValley, bringing slightly higher temperatures and humidity for \nWednesday and Thursday, with highs nearing 80F.\n\nLight rain may develop as early as Tuesday evening within a \nzone of warm advection associated with a lifting warm front. \nHowever, the primary round of impactful precipitation is \nprojected for Wednesday through Friday as the southwestern U.S. \nshortwave tracks across the Southern Plains and Mid-Mississippi \nValley. Although severe weather parameters appear marginal, \nheavy rainfall remains a concern toward the end of next week as \nPolo undergoes extratropical transition across the mid- \nlatitudes. Current NBM guidance highlights a 20-40% probability \nof rainfall exceeding 2 inches across our area from Wednesday \nmorning to Friday morning.",
